The convergence diagnostics do not suggest major sampling problems. Values of Rhat near 1 and adequate effective sample sizes indicate that the posterior chains mixed well enough for the current analysis. This does not guarantee a perfect model, but it does support the basic reliability of the posterior summaries.

12.1 flocker Data Formatting
flocker expects:
Observation matrix: one row per site, one column per survey
Unit-level covariate: one row per site
Event-level covariates: one matrix per survey-level predictor

So we are going to do a lot of pivoting..

# 
obs_mat &lt;- survey_df |&gt;
  arrange(site_id, survey_id) |&gt;
  select(site_id, survey_id, detected) |&gt;
  pivot_wider(
    names_from = survey_id,
    values_from = detected
  ) |&gt;
  tibble::column_to_rownames("site_id") |&gt;
  as.matrix()

# 
unit_covs &lt;- survey_df |&gt;
  distinct(site_id, region, site_age_z) |&gt;
  arrange(site_id) |&gt;
  mutate(
    region = factor(region, levels = c("North", "South", "Central", "West"))
  ) |&gt;
  select(region, site_age_z)

# 
cloud_mat &lt;- survey_df |&gt;
  arrange(site_id, survey_id) |&gt;
  select(site_id, survey_id, cloud_cover_z) |&gt;
  pivot_wider(
    names_from = survey_id,
    values_from = cloud_cover_z
  ) |&gt;
  tibble::column_to_rownames("site_id") |&gt;
  as.matrix()

sensor_mat &lt;- survey_df |&gt;
  arrange(site_id, survey_id) |&gt;
  select(site_id, survey_id, sensor_advanced) |&gt;
  pivot_wider(
    names_from = survey_id,
    values_from = sensor_advanced
  ) |&gt;
  tibble::column_to_rownames("site_id") |&gt;
  as.matrix()

flocker_data &lt;- make_flocker_data_static(
  obs = obs_mat,
  unit_covs = unit_covs,
  event_covs = list(
    cloud_cover_z = cloud_mat,
    sensor_advanced = sensor_mat
  )
)

